# How a runner command pins its backend, read by the fleet's `fleet-worker.sh execution leg`
# (lukstafi/ludics-lite#766 and #773; grammar in ludics-lite's issue-wave/references/executions.md,
# *A leg on another box*). The leg reads this file on the box AT THE COMMIT under test, before the
# worktree moves. A leg that may hold the GPU -- any leg not declaring `--cpu`, a measurement's
# included -- whose runner satisfies none of the pins below is refused before anything runs
# (gh-ocannl-1542). Four wave legs meant for CUDA or HIP set no backend, resolved to cc, and
# reported green (wave-20261021, for gh-ocannl-1340, 1452, 1432 and 1456).
#
# A pin shows only that the command NAMES a backend, never that the run held it: that check is
# `tools/test-run.sh`'s own `--expect-backend` (gh-ocannl-1433), so a GPU leg passes both:
#   -- env OCANNL_BACKEND=cuda tools/test-run.sh run --expect-backend cuda build @<dir>/runtest-<name>
# (the runner takes the spaced `--expect-backend B` only; it refuses `--expect-backend=B`, which
# the pin would admit, as a misplaced option, so that spelling fails loudly rather than passing).
# `bisect` and `repeat` take no `--expect-backend`; the `env` pin covers them. A cc-only leg either
# declares `--cpu` or names cc (`env OCANNL_BACKEND=cc`). Only argv words count: a pin inside a
# `sh -c` string, or an environment the caller exported, does not.
#
# The grammar is a fail-closed allowlist: `# comment` lines, blank lines, `pin-flag <option>` and
# `pin-env <NAME>`. Any other line refuses EVERY leg, `--cpu` ones included.

pin-flag --expect-backend
pin-env OCANNL_BACKEND
